{
  "image_name": "42d30ad2dd2d9948747ecf079c05c891.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares four main approaches for studying how plants respond to rainfall. It is organized as a conceptual overview, showing a progression from controlled experiments and observations to simpler and more complex models, with each approach paired with its main strengths and weaknesses.\n\nOne part shows a field manipulation experiment under a rainout shelter, where different plots receive different rainfall patterns, shifting from many small events to fewer larger events. This emphasizes precise control and direct estimation of rainfall effects, but also highlights limited scale, cost, and incomplete control of other environmental factors.\n\nAnother part presents observation-driven analysis, using plant and rain measurements from multiple places or years, together with satellites and eddy covariance towers. This approach captures integrated plant responses over large spatial scales, but it is harder to separate rainfall effects from other climatic influences.\n\nThe modeling side contrasts minimalist process models with numerical process models. Minimalist models use stochastic rainfall and coupled equations to link water balance and plant function, giving interpretable, scenario-based predictions but with simplified processes. Numerical models represent interactions among atmosphere, vegetation, and soil over realistic landscapes and biomes, offering broader Earth-system detail while remaining difficult to interpret and still simplifying biology and soils.",
